{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg5uiobxSrVB4FhnmVtB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Method.** I used the standard second-order, low-spin d⁶ expressions for the two spin-allowed transitions from ¹A₁g. These are quoted from memory and are approximate. I did not check them against the full Tanabe–Sugano matrices.\n\n- ν₁(¹A₁g→¹T₁g) = Δ − C + 86B²/Δ\n- ν₂(¹A₁g→¹T₂g) = Δ + 16B − C + 90B²/Δ\n\nHere Δ = 10Dq and C = 4.5B.\n\n**Solution.**\n\n1. Subtracting the two expressions gives ν₂ − ν₁ = 16B + 4B²/Δ = 23 529 − 16 611 = 6918 cm⁻¹.\n2. Neglecting the small second term gives B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹. Iterating with Δ ≈ 17 650 cm⁻¹ gives a correction of about 42 cm⁻¹ from the 4B²/Δ term, so B ≈ (6918 − 42)/16 ≈ 430 cm⁻¹.\n3. Then C = 4.5B ≈ 1934 cm⁻¹.\n4. From ν₁: Δ = 16 611 + C − 86B²/Δ = 16 611 + 1934 − 900 ≈ 17 645 cm⁻¹. The 86B²/Δ term is about 900 cm⁻¹ and was iterated to convergence.\n\n**Result.** Δ ≈ 17 650 cm⁻¹, so Dq ≈ 1765 cm⁻¹, with B ≈ 430 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 1934 cm⁻¹. B is about 40% of the free-ion value for Co³⁺, which indicates substantial covalency.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1765, \"B\": 430, \"C\": 1934, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Low-spin d6 fit of 1T1 and 1T2 bands with C/B=4.5 using second-order expressions.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":820,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"615bec95-ab6c-4c4f-ad3a-8a2ab71b556e","uuid":"d1d398e0-2d5b-4164-937f-fb3b8ef2ca7a","timestamp":"2026-11-03T13:37:41.039Z","request_id":"req_011Cgg5uiK6EXcMKpRq4ty2L"}
